WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.402
I ran two AI models as listeners.

00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:06.306
I gave them clean clips and
clips with a sniff added,

00:00:06.886 --> 00:00:09.589
and both said yes, there
is an unnatural pause,

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:11.110
every single time.

00:00:12.591 --> 00:00:15.354
A listener that always says
yes tells you nothing.

00:00:16.695 --> 00:00:19.117
This video is about the
models I use instead.

00:00:20.618 --> 00:00:23.620
To see why that matters, here
is what this series builds:

00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:29.084
a video with narration, where every
stage hands one job to one model.

00:00:30.324 --> 00:00:32.446
A few of them run on a
graphics card at home,

00:00:33.026 --> 00:00:34.767
a few on an ordinary processor,

00:00:35.348 --> 00:00:37.889
and the rest are Claude
models in the cloud.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.293
I name each one, say what it is
good at, and say what it cannot do.

00:00:44.573 --> 00:00:48.516
For the main ones I also say what
the license page said, and when.

00:00:49.877 --> 00:00:54.560
These are the models in early November
of 2026, and they will change.

00:00:56.099 --> 00:01:00.000
Start with the voice, because
everything else follows its timing.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.482
Audio8, a small open model, runs on my own
graphics card and clones my voice from

00:01:07.642 --> 00:01:08.602
one recording of me.

00:01:09.362 --> 00:01:13.103
Because it handles one request
at a time, one card is enough.

00:01:14.624 --> 00:01:18.585
Its model card says Apache
2.0 for the code and weights,

00:01:19.185 --> 00:01:21.885
as an AI agent read it on
the first of November,

00:01:22.465 --> 00:01:23.726
so check the page yourself.

00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:27.507
The card does not describe
the training data,

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:31.028
and the license does not say
whose voice you may clone,

00:01:31.548 --> 00:01:32.928
so clone only your own.

00:01:34.468 --> 00:01:40.630
The model adds no watermark, so I put a line in
each description saying the voice is synthetic.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.492
A synthetic voice can slip, by
dropping a word or adding a sound,

00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:49.473
so two more models check every recording.

00:01:50.554 --> 00:01:54.275
An aligner finds exactly when
each word starts and ends,

00:01:54.856 --> 00:01:57.797
and that is what lets every
picture line up with the audio.

00:01:58.997 --> 00:02:03.779
A speech recognizer writes down what it
hears, and I compare that with the script.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.041
Both run on a regular processor,

00:02:07.621 --> 00:02:11.442
and an AI agent read their license
pages on October twenty-eighth:

00:02:12.043 --> 00:02:14.944
BSD for the aligner,
MIT for the recognizer.

00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:18.305
The recognizer has one blind spot:

00:02:18.886 --> 00:02:21.267
if the voice adds an
extra sound to a word,

00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:23.407
it still writes the right word,

00:02:23.888 --> 00:02:25.148
and it never hears a sniff.

00:02:26.568 --> 00:02:29.129
That blind spot made me
want a third listener,

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.430
one that can tell how the voice sounds,

00:02:31.789 --> 00:02:32.729
not only what it says.

00:02:34.251 --> 00:02:37.371
So I ran two AI models
that take audio as input,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.951
and a naturalness score,
on eighteen short clips,

00:02:41.451 --> 00:02:45.073
some clean and some with a sniff
or a loud word that I added.

00:02:46.693 --> 00:02:51.753
In my test, with my prompt, both models
said yes, unnatural pause, to every clip.

00:02:53.354 --> 00:02:57.735
The score gave the same number to the
clean clips and to every broken one.

00:02:59.015 --> 00:03:05.197
What is promising is plainer: flag a sound only when
it stands out from the rest of the same episode.

00:03:06.576 --> 00:03:09.176
That method found all
four sniffs I had added,

00:03:09.757 --> 00:03:11.897
with two false alarms on the other clips.

00:03:12.957 --> 00:03:14.777
That was one test on one voice,

00:03:15.338 --> 00:03:17.498
and I have not checked it against my ears,

00:03:18.118 --> 00:03:21.099
so everything it flags is only
a candidate that I listen to.

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:26.481
With the sound covered, the other
half of the video is what you see,

00:03:27.061 --> 00:03:31.523
and that is the one job where I pay
for the strongest model I can get.

00:03:33.204 --> 00:03:35.845
Claude Opus, one of Anthropic's models,

00:03:36.426 --> 00:03:39.107
writes each animation as
a small piece of code,

00:03:39.687 --> 00:03:43.389
and a second Opus session reviews
the frames and looks for faults.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:49.512
I compared it with the smaller Sonnet
model on two scenes with the same brief.

00:03:50.492 --> 00:03:53.213
On the denser scene Opus
took about ten times longer,

00:03:53.793 --> 00:03:55.814
but I judged its animations much better,

00:03:56.395 --> 00:03:58.055
so the shorter time was not worth it.

00:03:59.196 --> 00:04:03.898
Two scenes is a small sample, so that
is my judgement, not a benchmark.

00:04:05.438 --> 00:04:07.479
Those models do not coordinate themselves,

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:10.200
so one more program sits above them:

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.943
Claude Code, which runs on my machine
and calls Claude models in the cloud.

00:04:16.703 --> 00:04:20.925
It runs the stages in order and starts
the picture authors and the reviewers.

00:04:22.445 --> 00:04:24.746
Then it runs every layout
check itself again,

00:04:25.347 --> 00:04:31.289
to confirm each result, instead of trusting an
author who reports that its own work is clean.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.651
Each author sees only its own
brief and a few reference files,

00:04:37.251 --> 00:04:38.812
and each reviewer can only read,

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:41.793
so an author does not
review its own scenes.

00:04:43.374 --> 00:04:48.757
That is the whole cast, and the question
for you is which parts run on your machine

00:04:49.157 --> 00:04:50.138
and which you pay for.

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:53.140
Three models run on your machine:

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:58.843
the voice on a graphics card, and the
aligner and the recognizer on a processor.

00:05:00.103 --> 00:05:03.185
The writing and the pictures
need a paid Claude plan.

00:05:04.105 --> 00:05:07.807
When an AI agent read the pricing
page on the second of November,

00:05:08.388 --> 00:05:14.631
Claude Code and Opus 5.5 were listed as included
on the paid plans and not on the free one.

00:05:15.872 --> 00:05:20.774
I did not check the usage limits, and
plans change, so check the page yourself.

00:05:22.135 --> 00:05:27.098
I did not try other chat models, and I
have not yet timed or priced an episode.

00:05:28.579 --> 00:05:31.301
That is the cast: one voice, two ears,

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:35.205
a writer and a producer, and a set
of listeners that did not make it.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:40.130
Next, I'll set up your machine
and make your first render.
